WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.182
Before any AI-assisted cloud assessment
begins,

2
00:00:05.262 --> 00:00:09.052
the team must define what is authorized.

3
00:00:09.132 --> 00:00:15.820
Scope should identify the cloud providers,
accounts, subscriptions,

4
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:22.588
projects, tenants, organizations, folders,
regions, environments,

5
00:00:22.668 --> 00:00:26.123
and services included in the review.

6
00:00:26.203 --> 00:00:29.659
It should also identify exclusions.

7
00:00:29.739 --> 00:00:36.873
A model should never be allowed to infer
that a related system is in scope just

8
00:00:36.953 --> 00:00:39.962
because it appears in evidence.

9
00:00:40.042 --> 00:00:44.835
Assessment boundaries should address
ownership.

10
00:00:44.915 --> 00:00:51.826
The environment may include internal
systems, client-owned resources,

11
00:00:51.906 --> 00:00:59.263
vendor-managed services, shared platforms,
and third-party integrations.

12
00:00:59.343 --> 00:01:07.369
Each category can have different access
rules, evidence limits, and approval paths.

13
00:01:07.449 --> 00:01:15.586
The assessment should respect those
boundaries even when the technical architecture

14
00:01:15.666 --> 00:01:17.004
crosses them.

15
00:01:17.084 --> 00:01:24.329
Rules of engagement translate
authorization into operating instructions.

16
00:01:24.409 --> 00:01:28.979
They should describe what data may be
processed,

17
00:01:29.059 --> 00:01:35.859
what evidence may be shared with AI tools,
which activities are allowed,

18
00:01:35.939 --> 00:01:40.955
which actions are prohibited, who approves
changes,

19
00:01:41.035 --> 00:01:45.494
and how sensitive information must be
handled.

20
00:01:45.574 --> 00:01:52.708
This is especially important for IAM
exports, logs, architecture records,

21
00:01:52.788 --> 00:01:59.364
and vulnerability summaries that may
include secrets, identifiers,

22
00:01:59.444 --> 00:02:01.228
or regulated data.

23
00:02:01.308 --> 00:02:07.884
Production and development systems should
be handled deliberately.

24
00:02:07.964 --> 00:02:13.203
Development environments often have weaker
controls,

25
00:02:13.283 --> 00:02:19.414
but they may still hold sensitive data or
privileged identities.

26
00:02:19.494 --> 00:02:27.520
Production environments may require
stricter handling because mistakes can affect

27
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:30.944
customers or business operations.

28
00:02:31.024 --> 00:02:39.049
The assessment should document environment
differences rather than treating every

29
00:02:39.129 --> 00:02:42.028
cloud resource as equivalent.

30
00:02:42.108 --> 00:02:48.796
Scope control protects both the
organization and the assessment team.

31
00:02:48.876 --> 00:02:55.452
It prevents unauthorized analysis, reduces
accidental disclosure,

32
00:02:55.532 --> 00:03:00.214
and keeps findings tied to defensible
authority.

33
00:03:00.294 --> 00:03:04.418
AI should help within the defined
boundary.

34
00:03:04.498 --> 00:03:11.521
It should not broaden the boundary, create
assumptions about permission,

35
00:03:11.601 --> 00:03:17.620
or suggest actions that require approval
the team does not have.
